<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">

<!--
  Syxx polkit action (P-02). Installed by build_deb.sh to
  /usr/share/polkit-1/actions/app.syxx.policy.

  WHY THIS EXISTS
    pkexec was ALREADY load-bearing on Linux before this file: Syxx.Kit/UpdateService.cs:409
    shells `/usr/bin/pkexec dpkg -i <package>` to install an update. So graphical escalation
    was never missing — it was undeclared. Without an action file pkexec falls back to
    org.freedesktop.policykit.exec, whose dialog says only "Authentication is required to run
    `/usr/bin/syxx' as the super user". The user is asked to grant root to a program with no
    stated purpose. Declaring the action replaces that with the message below.

  WHY pkexec AND NOT setcap (owner decision 2)
    `setcap cap_net_raw+ep /opt/syxx/cli/Syxx` would let Cage capture with no prompt at all —
    and would hand raw-socket capability to EVERY local user, permanently, from the moment the
    package is installed. For a forensics tool that is a worse trade than one prompt per
    capture. This is the same reasoning scripts/syxx-capture-hardware-id.sh already applied
    when it rejected a setuid helper and a root daemon.

  WHY auth_admin AND NOT auth_admin_keep
    `_keep` caches the authorization for ~5 minutes, so a second capture inside that window
    starts silently. The decision is pkexec-PER-CAPTURE: every privileged capture is a thing
    the user consciously authorized. Do not "improve" this to _keep.

  SCOPE — STATED PLAINLY BECAUSE IT IS BROADER THAN THE NAME SUGGESTS
    exec.path is /usr/bin/syxx, the whole CLI. One authorization therefore runs ANY syxx
    subcommand as root, not just `cage`. That is the same surface `sudo syxx cage` already
    has, so this is not a regression — but it is not least-privilege either. Narrowing it
    needs a dedicated capture-only helper verb on the CLI (a C#-side change, not a packaging
    one); see the E3 report's CROSS-SEAT REQUESTS.

  CALLERS MUST INVOKE EXACTLY `/usr/bin/syxx`
    polkit matches the action to the path pkexec was given. `pkexec /opt/syxx/cli/Syxx cage`
    resolves to a DIFFERENT path, matches no action here, and silently falls back to the
    generic org.freedesktop.policykit.exec dialog. Use /usr/bin/syxx.
-->
<policyconfig>

  <vendor>Syxx</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://syxx.app</vendor_url>
  <icon_name>syxx</icon_name>

  <action id="app.syxx.run-privileged">
    <description>Run a privileged Syxx investigation</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to capture DNS traffic on this machine. Syxx records the
    packets it captures as evidence; it does not change your network configuration.</message>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>auth_admin</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>auth_admin</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/bin/syxx</annotate>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.allow_gui">true</annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
